{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "param": "data_width",
          "low": 14,
          "high": 18
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "n_iter",
          "low": 12,
          "high": 18
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "angle_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "frac_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "rounding",
          "low": null,
          "high": null
        ,
          "choices": ["trunc", "round"]
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "why": "iterative is the only family that can plausibly hit 50 MSPS: with 1 result every N+3 cycles, throughput = Fmax/(N+3). To reach 50 MSPS we need Fmax >= 50*(N+3) MHz, so N must be small (N~12-18 gives Fmax targets of ~750-1100 MHz, which is aggressive but the only way this family meets the constraint; larger N makes it infeasible). Accuracy must be <= 2^-12, which for CORDIC is driven by n_iter and the guard bits, so I keep n_iter in 12-18 and data_width 14-18 (W=14 gives 12 fractional output bits, the minimum to even represent 2^-12). Small angle_guard/frac_guard keep the datapath narrow to push Fmax up and cut power. This is the high-risk/high-reward corner: if it meets throughput it will be the lowest-power point on the front."
    },
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "param": "data_width",
          "low": 14,
          "high": 18
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "n_iter",
          "low": 12,
          "high": 24
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "angle_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "frac_guard",
          "low": 0,
          "high": 2
        ,
          "choices": null
        },
        {
          "param": "rounding",
          "low": null,
          "high": null
        ,
          "choices": ["trunc", "round"]
        },
        {
          "param": "k",
          "low": 2,
          "high": 8
        ,
          "choices": null
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "why": "unrolled_k produces 1 result every ceil(N/k)+3 cycles, so for the same N it needs a much lower Fmax than iterative to hit 50 MSPS (e.g. N=18, k=4 -> 8 cycles/result -> Fmax >= 400 MHz, far more realistic). This is the more likely feasible family and the main carrier of the Pareto front. I widen n_iter to 24 and add k (2-8) so the search can trade off unroll depth against the number of micro-rotations: larger k raises per-cycle logic (more area/power, lower Fmax) but cuts cycles/result. Keeping data_width 14-18 and small guards preserves the accuracy-vs-power trade-off. Together with iterative this spans the low-power end (iterative, if feasible) and the realistic feasible bulk (unrolled_k)."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The binding constraint is throughput >= 50 MSPS, and only iterative and unrolled_k are available to me. Neither is a 1-result/cycle family, so both must run at high Fmax; unrolled_k is far more likely to be feasible because k micro-rotations/cycle cuts the cycles-per-result. I therefore split the budget evenly: half on iterative to probe whether the lowest-power corner (small N, minimal guards) can actually reach 50 MSPS, and half on unrolled_k to map the realistic feasible front across k and n_iter. Accuracy (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) is the trade-off objective, so I keep data_width at the minimum that can represent 12 fractional bits (14) up to 18, n_iter in the 12-24 band where CORDIC error crosses 2^-12, and angle/frac guards small (0-2) to keep the datapath narrow for Fmax and power. Rounding is left as a 2-choice to let the solver pick the cheaper option. This focuses the 100 evals on the region where the power/accuracy trade-off actually lives rather than wasting budget on obviously-too-accurate or obviously-too-slow corners."
}

{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "next_plan": {
    "families": [
      {
        "budget_share": 0.5,
        "family": "pipelined",
        "ranges": [
          {
            "choices": [
              "trunc",
              "round"
            ],
            "high": 4,
            "low": 0,
            "param": "frac_guard"
          },
          {
            "choices": [
              "trunc",
              "round"
            ],
            "high": 4,
            "low": -2,
            "param": "angle_guard"
          },
          {
            "choices": [
              "trunc",
              "round"
            ],
            "high": 16,
            "low": 14,
            "param": "n_iter"
          },
          {
            "choices": [
              "trunc",
              "round"
            ],
            "high": 18,
            "low": 16,
            "param": "data_width"
          }
        ]
      ,
        "why": "Round 1 proved the shared-datapath families (iterative, unrolled_k) cannot reach 50 MSPS: best throughput was 9.83 MSPS (iterative) and 7.15 MSPS (unrolled_k), a 5-7x shortfall. The accuracy constraint is already met by several iterative designs (best 14.11 bits vs required 12 bits), so accuracy is not the bottleneck. The pipelined family produces 1 result/cycle, which is the only architecture in the registry capable of reaching 50 MSPS throughput. I narrow the ranges to the accuracy-feasible region found in Round 1 (data_width 16-18, n_iter 14-16) to focus the budget on finding the minimum-power pipelined design that meets both constraints."
      }
    ]
  ,
    "rationale": "The throughput constraint (>= 50 MSPS) is the binding constraint. Round 1 showed iterative maxes out at 9.83 MSPS and unrolled_k at 7.15 MSPS, both far below 50. Only the pipelined and pipelined_m families produce 1 result/cycle and can plausibly reach 50 MSPS. I add pipelined first (simpler, fewer parameters) with ranges narrowed to the accuracy-feasible region (data_width 16-18, n_iter 14-16) identified in Round 1, where max_abs_err <= 2^-12 was already achieved. This focuses the 40 remaining evaluations on the only family that can satisfy throughput while maintaining accuracy."
  }
,
  "rationale": "Round 1 showed that neither iterative (max 9.83 MSPS) nor unrolled_k (max 7.15 MSPS) can reach the 50 MSPS throughput constraint. The accuracy constraint is already satisfiable (best 14.11 bits vs 12 required). The pipelined family is the only remaining architecture that produces 1 result/cycle and can plausibly meet 50 MSPS. I narrow its parameter ranges to the accuracy-feasible region found in Round 1 to maximize the chance of finding a feasible design within the remaining budget."
}
